  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Lingering showers in central Virginia through\nlate evening, then only isolated showers and thunderstorms \nSaturday and Sunday. \n\nA mid/upper trough axis remains to our west this evening, with\njet streak dynamics assisting in lift across the Mid Atlantic\ndespite a surface boundary pushing south of the area. A few\nshowers have continued to intermittently development where low\nlevel moisture and CAPE is greater across central Virginia.\nThese should diminish during the late evening hours. A \nsecondary front will push southward overnight, allowing lower \ndew points to spill southward. This drier air and brief uptick \nin winds may mitigate fog in most areas, but guidance does still\nshow river valley fog west of the Blue Ridge where winds should\nbe calm and air temperatures drop more efficiently.\n\nHigh pressure will pass north of the area Saturday and then off\nthe Mid Atlantic coast Sunday. Most areas should remain dry and\nseasonable Saturday, although an inverted surface trough may\nresult in isolated showers along the Allegheny Mountains.\nSoutherly flow returns Sunday, bringing increasing heat and\nhumidity. A passing shortwave trough may support a few showers\nand thunderstorms developing, especially closer to the MD/PA\nborder.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Hot and humid conditions next week with increasing \nthunderstorm chances by midweek.\n\nModels are generally in good agreement that a strong mid-level ridge \nbuilds across the southern U.S. and into the Mid-Atlantic during the \nearly to middle part of next week. Well above normal temperatures \nfor late August can be expected as highs reach the lower to mid 90s. \nHot and humid conditions are likely as heat indices reach the upper \n90s and possibly the lower 100s. \n\nA more notable mid-level disturbance will progress from the northern \nHigh Plains to the Northeast toward the middle of next week. The \nridge will be suppressed as a result, but it remains uncertain how \nmuch and how quickly this happens. The latest trend in the ensemble \nguidance is for the upper ridge over the Mid-South to be a bit \nfurther west. If this pans out, our area in the Mid-Atlantic will be \nin a more favorable location along the periphery of the ridge to be \nimpacted by passing MCSs. Eventually, by mid week it looks like the \nupper ridge retreats enough to allow the shortwave troughs to move \nover our area. Several of the AI models indicate increasing severe \npotential at some point Tuesday through Friday, which is certainly \npossible given the high instability that will be in place, along \nwith substantial upper-level support from any strong disturbance. \nIt's also possible a back door cold front tries to drop into the \narea by Wednesday or Thursday, leading to greater uncertainty in \ntemperatures.",
